1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,920
The school Saturday requires supraglottic airways and suction as well
as CPAP and capnography. This practical lesson joins those skills to
the assessment you already learned; it is not another isolated list of
devices.

2
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:26,088
We will follow one problem: can air move through the upper airway and
produce an adequate breath? Equipment is useful only when it solves
that problem without creating a more serious one.

3
00:00:26,088 --> 00:00:39,456
Prepare oxygen, a functioning suction unit, an appropriate bag and
mask, adjuncts, monitoring and a backup plan. Assign one person to
watch the patient while another prepares the next step.

4
00:00:39,456 --> 00:00:54,024
The explanation prepares you for supervised mannequin practice. The
actual school device, current procedure and instructor assessment
determine the skill sign-off; no procedure should be practised on an
unconsenting person.

5
00:00:54,024 --> 00:01:07,872
Responsiveness, breathing and circulation determine the immediate
pathway. Gasping is not normal breathing. A trained pulse check must
be brief and must not become an excuse for prolonged inaction.

6
00:01:07,872 --> 00:01:20,496
With a definite pulse but ineffective or absent breathing, open and
clear the airway and support breaths. Oxygen through a mask alone
cannot replace missing alveolar ventilation.

7
00:01:20,496 --> 00:01:34,752
When no definite pulse is found promptly in an unresponsive adult with
abnormal breathing, use the arrest pathway and bring an AED. Do not
wait to see whether naloxone or a higher oxygen flow will restore
circulation.

8
00:01:34,752 --> 00:01:46,368
A suspected foreign body creates a different problem. Forcing a
supraglottic device past an obstruction does not remove that
obstruction and may delay the intervention that is actually needed.

9
00:01:46,368 --> 00:01:58,392
This is the adult BLS bridge, not the full Week Five resuscitation
class. Activate help, begin high-quality compressions and use an AED
promptly when arrest is recognized.

10
00:01:58,392 --> 00:02:13,152
Compress the lower half of the sternum at one hundred to one hundred
twenty per minute, with adult depth about five to six centimetres,
full recoil and minimal interruptions. Follow the current trained
protocol and feedback device.

11
00:02:13,152 --> 00:02:26,904
Before an advanced airway, the standard adult BLS ratio is thirty
compressions to two breaths. Give breaths that produce visible chest
rise rather than two maximal squeezes. Keep the pause short.

12
00:02:26,904 --> 00:02:40,512
Clear everyone for AED analysis and shock, then resume compressions as
directed. Assign airway preparation to another rescuer so that
searching for equipment does not stop the lifesaving work already
under way.

13
00:02:40,512 --> 00:02:53,424
An effective cough and the ability to speak indicate some airflow.
Encourage coughing and monitor for deterioration rather than
immediately interrupting a useful cough with thrusts or a blind finger
sweep.

14
00:02:53,424 --> 00:03:07,848
For severe responsive adult or child choking, the current AHA sequence
starts with five back blows followed by five abdominal thrusts. Use
chest thrusts for late pregnancy or when the abdomen cannot be
encircled.

15
00:03:07,848 --> 00:03:20,904
An infant uses back blows and chest thrusts, never abdominal thrusts.
Support the infant as demonstrated in the current pediatric class; do
not copy adult hand placement onto a small infant.

16
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:37,968
The saved June examination guide accepts alternating two effective
techniques in sets of five for responsive adults and children. Keep
that named examination wording separate from the current AHA starting
sequence; ask the instructor which version governs the school station.

17
00:03:37,968 --> 00:03:52,320
When a responsive choking patient collapses, support the person to a
safe surface, activate help and begin CPR with compressions. The
collapse branch is not a reason to spend time repeating abdominal
thrusts on the floor.

18
00:03:52,320 --> 00:04:04,416
Each time the airway is opened for breaths, inspect the mouth. Remove
an object only when it is visible and can be reached safely. A blind
sweep can push it deeper or injure tissue.

19
00:04:04,416 --> 00:04:23,016
The saved examination guide also gives a separate unwitnessed-
unconscious obstruction branch: one breath, reposition and another
attempt if no entry, then compressions when ventilation remains
blocked. Do not confuse that branch with a witnessed conscious-to-
unconscious collapse.

20
00:04:23,016 --> 00:04:39,480
Continue reassessment after an object is expelled. Persistent stridor,
pain, ineffective breathing or reduced consciousness still requires
support and urgent assessment; removal of the visible object is not
proof that all danger has ended.

21
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:52,512
Loss of muscle tone lets upper-airway structures narrow the passage.
Snoring may improve when the mandible is lifted; simply pressing a
mask onto the face does not correct that internal obstruction.

22
00:04:52,512 --> 00:05:08,496
Use the jaw and head-positioning techniques taught for the clinical
situation. With suspected cervical injury, start with a jaw thrust and
spinal precautions, but do not accept a closed airway merely to keep
an ineffective position unchanged.

23
00:05:08,496 --> 00:05:21,744
Body habitus can require support beneath the head and upper torso
rather than one thin pillow under the occiput. The useful position is
the one that improves access and airflow within injury precautions.

24
00:05:21,744 --> 00:05:37,056
After any repositioning, check the result. If chest rise remains
absent, reassess the passage, secretions, seal, obstruction and
equipment instead of assuming that correct-looking posture guarantees
ventilation.

25
00:05:37,056 --> 00:05:50,040
A suction unit in its bag is not yet ready. During the equipment
check, inspect its power, collection chamber, tubing, connectors and
catheter, then test function using the manufacturer method.

26
00:05:50,040 --> 00:06:05,040
A rigid oral tip provides controlled access to visible pooled fluid. A
soft catheter has different uses and depth limits; it is not
automatically an invitation to advance into a nose, trachea or gastric
channel.

27
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:19,296
Choose vacuum settings for the actual patient, procedure and
equipment. A value printed for one specialized i-gel procedure is not
the universal setting for every oral, pediatric or tracheal suction
task.

28
00:06:19,296 --> 00:06:33,624
Place the operating suction within reach at the head and prepare
ventilation support before beginning. Use the current infection
precautions for secretion exposure; gloves alone do not protect eyes
and airways from splashes.

29
00:06:33,624 --> 00:06:46,608
Wet gurgling and visible fluid suggest contamination that can obstruct
airflow or be aspirated. Open the mouth, inspect and use the oral
suction technique demonstrated with the actual catheter.

30
00:06:46,608 --> 00:06:59,760
Follow the catheter-specific insertion depth and activation method. Do
not sweep blindly into the back of the throat, lever on the teeth or
repeatedly traumatize tissue in search of a perfectly dry mouth.

31
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:10,944
If a solid object is visible, use the appropriate trained removal
method. A small blocked suction opening cannot be repaired by pushing
the catheter farther into the patient.

32
00:07:10,944 --> 00:07:25,920
Restore oxygenation and ventilation promptly after clearing. With
ongoing large-volume contamination, coordinate patient positioning,
repeated clearance and additional help rather than alternating long
periods with no effective breaths.

33
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:41,448
Nancy gives maximum oral suction attempts of fifteen seconds for an
adult, ten for a child and five for an infant. Do not keep suction on
until that clock expires when the airway has already cleared or the
patient is deteriorating.

34
00:07:41,448 --> 00:07:55,800
Suction interrupts oxygen delivery and can trigger gagging, trauma or
a vagal response. Stop and reassess when the patient shows harm, and
restore required oxygenation and ventilation between attempts.

35
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:08,664
If the unit stops collecting fluid, distinguish a clear airway from a
clogged catheter, full canister, leak or power failure. Check the
patient first and the entire suction path second.

36
00:08:08,664 --> 00:08:22,488
These oral timing figures are not copied into every specialized
procedure. The i-gel port method is a separately specified, depth-
limited pharyngeal technique, not unrestricted continuous suction.

37
00:08:22,488 --> 00:08:32,688
The oropharyngeal airway holds the tongue away from the posterior
passage. It does not ventilate, seal the larynx or protect the patient
from aspiration.

38
00:08:32,688 --> 00:08:43,320
Use it in an unresponsive patient who does not have a gag response and
tolerates insertion. Do not deliberately provoke a deep gag to prove
that an airway is safe.

39
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:57,768
An airway that is too short, too long or poorly placed can worsen
obstruction. Measure the selected device and use the demonstrated
technique; the adult rotation method is not automatically appropriate
for an infant or child.

40
00:08:57,768 --> 00:09:09,168
Reassess airflow and a test breath after placement while maintaining
positioning. Remove it if gagging or vomiting occurs, clear the airway
and choose another appropriate means of support.

41
00:09:09,168 --> 00:09:21,744
A nasopharyngeal airway can be useful when an adjunct is needed but an
oral airway cannot be tolerated or placed. It still does not prevent
aspiration or remove the need for manual jaw support.

42
00:09:21,744 --> 00:09:34,704
Current PR zero seven measures the candidate in its neutral curve from
nostril to tragus. The textbook offers other landmarks; name the
actual procedure rather than silently mixing the methods.

43
00:09:34,704 --> 00:09:46,560
Lubricate the outside without blocking the lumen and advance gently
backward along the nasal passage, not upward toward the bridge of the
nose. Stop rather than forcing resistance.

44
00:09:46,560 --> 00:10:01,176
Significant maxillofacial injury, particularly a Le Fort pattern, is
an exclusion in the current procedure. Inspect the actual injury and
choose an appropriate alternative rather than relying on a little-
finger size shortcut.

45
00:10:01,176 --> 00:10:11,424
A flange resting at the nostril proves only that the proximal end is
there. Check whether snoring, airflow and ventilation have actually
improved.

46
00:10:11,424 --> 00:10:26,496
Epistaxis can introduce new airway contamination. Current PR zero
seven permits leaving the device if it is not causing compromise; if
it obstructs or compromises the airway, remove it and use protective
support.

47
00:10:26,496 --> 00:10:41,448
The older textbook describes suction through a nasal airway. Current
BCEHS PR zero seven explicitly prohibits PCP suction through its
lumen. Treat this as a source correction, not as an optional style
preference.

48
00:10:41,448 --> 00:10:51,504
After repositioning or transfer, repeat the patient-based check. An
adjunct can move or become obstructed even while its flange appears
unchanged.

49
00:10:51,504 --> 00:11:05,064
Check the bag, patient valve, mask, reservoir and oxygen connection as
one system. A reservoir full of oxygen does not help when the patient
valve is obstructed or the mask is disconnected.

50
00:11:05,064 --> 00:11:20,616
Use the appropriate equipment size and manufacturer assembly. The
actual oxygen concentration depends on flow, reservoir, seal and
patient demand; a fifteen-litre setting is not proof of exactly one
hundred percent delivery.

51
00:11:20,616 --> 00:11:33,840
Add the required compatible filter and capnography connector without
creating a kink, unapproved arrangement or excessive dead space for a
small patient. Equipment compatibility is part of preparation.

52
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:46,752
Test the system before it is urgently needed and keep suction
accessible. In a real failure, quickly restore an effective backup
instead of repeatedly squeezing an assembly you have not verified.

53
00:11:46,752 --> 00:11:59,712
One rescuer must otherwise open the airway, hold a seal and squeeze
the bag with only two hands. Two-provider technique separates those
jobs and makes failure easier to recognize and correct.

54
00:11:59,712 --> 00:12:12,648
The person at the head supports the mandible with a practiced two-hand
grip and holds the mask over nose and mouth. Lift the jaw toward the
mask rather than crushing the mask downward into soft tissue.

55
00:12:12,648 --> 00:12:25,584
The second person gives a controlled breath while watching the exposed
chest. State whether there is slight definite rise, a leak, resistance
or no effect so that the head provider can adjust immediately.

56
00:12:25,584 --> 00:12:36,984
Avoid pressure over the eyes or beneath the chin that closes the
passage. A louder squeak or stronger bag squeeze is not proof that a
poor mask seal has been corrected.

57
00:12:36,984 --> 00:12:50,928
A breath needs enough volume to expand the chest, not enough to empty
an adult bag. Nancy contains a complete-squeeze instruction in its
skill drill; do not adopt that wording as a universal volume target.

58
00:12:50,928 --> 00:13:03,288
Give the breath smoothly over about one second in the named BLS
framework and watch for slight definite chest rise. Then release
pressure and allow the chest to fall before the next breath.

59
00:13:03,288 --> 00:13:17,304
Fast or excessive breaths increase airway pressure, gastric inflation
and regurgitation risk. Positive pressure can also reduce venous
return, especially in a patient who is already poorly perfused.

60
00:13:17,304 --> 00:13:28,056
Use the least pressure and volume that achieve the clinical goal. If
the chest does not rise, troubleshoot the reason rather than
automatically squeezing harder and faster.

61
00:13:28,056 --> 00:13:40,776
Look at the patient with every breath. Similar chest rise on both
sides, air movement and appropriate resistance give immediate
feedback, while abnormal asymmetry requires reassessment.

62
00:13:40,776 --> 00:13:53,160
Pulse oximetry can lag and may initially look reassuring despite poor
ventilation. Do not wait for saturation to fall before correcting
absent chest rise or a blocked passage.

63
00:13:53,160 --> 00:14:08,496
Capnography adds information about exhaled carbon dioxide, but a low
value also depends on perfusion, leak and sampling. Interpret the
waveform with chest movement and the clinical state, not as an
isolated green number.

64
00:14:08,496 --> 00:14:20,904
Recheck immediately after moving the head, adding an adjunct,
transferring the patient or changing providers. An effective breath
five minutes ago does not certify the breath being given now.

65
00:14:20,904 --> 00:14:33,144
When the chest does not rise, announce ineffective ventilation and
request help. Reopen the airway, clear contamination and correct the
seal while another rescuer checks the connected system.

66
00:14:33,144 --> 00:14:48,672
If there is almost no bag resistance with an audible leak, focus on
the interface and disconnected components. If resistance is
unexpectedly high, consider obstruction, device blockage and patient
causes without forcing pressure.

67
00:14:48,672 --> 00:15:01,872
A correctly placed adjunct can help functional obstruction, but a
foreign body or pathology must follow its own pathway. A supraglottic
device is not a cure for every cause of difficult bagging.

68
00:15:01,872 --> 00:15:14,016
Restore the best effective basic support and obtain advanced help when
needed. Do not keep repeating a failed maneuver because the next
station on the school schedule happens to be SGA insertion.

69
00:15:14,016 --> 00:15:28,296
The current adult BLS respiratory-arrest rate is one breath every six
seconds, or ten per minute, with a definite pulse. The older textbook
adult range is not silently relabelled as that same recommendation.

70
00:15:28,296 --> 00:15:42,960
For infants and children with a pulse but absent or inadequate
breathing, current AHA and AAP use twenty to thirty breaths per
minute. This is not the older twelve-to-twenty range, and newborn
resuscitation is a separate framework.

71
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:56,448
During arrest, airway status and the resuscitation protocol determine
coordination with compressions. Do not apply rescue-breathing numbers
to a compression ratio or mix adult and pediatric procedures.

72
00:15:56,448 --> 00:16:10,080
In the school lab, state the named framework and then demonstrate an
appropriate breath with feedback. A correct metronome rate cannot
compensate for a leaking mask, an obstructed passage or excessive
volume.

73
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:22,680
The i-gel is a supraglottic device: it creates an interface around the
laryngeal inlet rather than passing through the vocal cords. That
distinction explains both its usefulness and its limits.

74
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:34,560
Its cuff is shaped to fit the anatomy and is not inflated with a
syringe. Do not copy the inflation instructions for a King airway or
an inflatable laryngeal mask onto this device.

75
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:47,400
A gastric channel does not make the airway aspiration-proof. It also
does not grant a PCP permission to insert a gastric tube; current
local pharyngeal suction is a specifically limited procedure.

76
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:57,888
The question is whether it safely improves airway management for this
patient, not whether it is more advanced than a mask. Continue
effective basic support while preparing it.

77
00:16:57,888 --> 00:17:10,248
Current PR zero eight includes patients unable to protect the airway
because of reduced consciousness. Outside cardiac arrest, its
additional PCP training or sign-off conditions still apply.

78
00:17:10,248 --> 00:17:27,648
Screen for a foreign body or pathological obstruction, active
vomiting, caustic ingestion, relevant airway or neck trauma and
inability to open the mouth. Anticipated high inspiratory pressure is
also a warning about likely seal difficulty.

79
00:17:27,648 --> 00:17:38,280
The current procedure requires confirming that the patient can be
ventilated by BVM first. Do not use an i-gel as a blind attempt to
bypass a suspected obstruction.

80
00:17:38,280 --> 00:17:51,120
State the backup before insertion: if placement is ineffective, return
promptly to the effective basic method and call for appropriate help.
That plan prevents fixation on a device that is failing.

81
00:17:51,120 --> 00:18:02,448
Use the weight range printed on the actual model together with
anatomy. Adult size ranges overlap; that is a reason to assess fit,
not a reason to choose by colour alone.

82
00:18:02,448 --> 00:18:14,304
Inspect the device, connector and channels and prepare the compatible
ventilation circuit. Its protective cradle is packaging, never an
introducer to be inserted into the mouth.

83
00:18:14,304 --> 00:18:26,664
Apply the required thin water-based lubricant layer without leaving a
lump that obstructs the airway opening. Keep contaminated fingers and
damaged equipment out of the prepared patient-contact surface.

84
00:18:26,664 --> 00:18:40,176
The manufacturer flash card is only a reminder and explicitly is not
its full instructions for use. During supervised practice, identify
the actual model and read its complete instructions before the
station.

85
00:18:40,176 --> 00:18:49,944
Prepare everything before interrupting effective ventilation. Position
the patient within injury precautions and open the mouth using the
taught technique.

86
00:18:49,944 --> 00:19:03,480
For the described i-gel, the outlet faces the chin and the soft
leading tip enters toward the hard palate. Advance with a continuous
gentle movement along that contour until definite resistance.

87
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:17,760
Do not repeatedly force or twist against resistance and do not inflate
a nonexistent cuff. A failed attempt should trigger reoxygenation,
reassessment and the actual attempt limits taught with the device.

88
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:29,448
Connect the prepared ventilation circuit and check the patient
immediately. Reaching a printed depth mark is not the final step; an
effective breath is the next decisive observation.

89
00:19:29,448 --> 00:19:43,464
Confirm that breaths produce chest rise and air entry without a major
leak. Listen and look for evidence of gastric inflation or ineffective
delivery; a seated connector alone is not confirmation.

90
00:19:43,464 --> 00:19:57,864
Use waveform capnography where available together with the clinical
examination. A waveform adds evidence of exhaled gas but does not turn
an SGA into a cuffed tracheal tube or guarantee protection from
aspiration.

91
00:19:57,864 --> 00:20:11,568
Secure the device with the supplied approved holder and reassess
afterward. Current PR zero eight specifically says not to use a Thomas
tube holder for this purpose, despite older materials showing
different advice.

92
00:20:11,568 --> 00:20:25,152
After lifting, loading or moving the head, check the patient, device
position, connections and waveform again. Fixation is intended to
reduce displacement, not to eliminate the need to look for it.

93
00:20:25,152 --> 00:20:38,304
If the i-gel does not ventilate effectively, announce the failure
rather than explaining it away as a low monitor reading. Check chest
movement, seal, depth, connections and contamination.

94
00:20:38,304 --> 00:20:50,328
Use only the gentle corrective maneuvers actually taught for that
device. Forcing it deeper or increasing pressure indefinitely can
worsen trauma, leakage and gastric inflation.

95
00:20:50,328 --> 00:21:05,472
When it is ineffective, return to the previously effective BVM method
as required and request higher-level help. Continuing to ventilate
through an unsuccessful interface is not perseverance; it is continued
failed delivery.

96
00:21:05,472 --> 00:21:17,544
Also reconsider patient causes, including obstruction, bronchospasm
and chest injury. A new airway device cannot correct every problem
downstream in the lungs or circulation.

97
00:21:17,544 --> 00:21:30,672
Current PR zero eight permits a modified pharyngeal suction approach
through the i-gel port during cardiac arrest. This does not authorize
gastric intubation or routine unrestricted suction through that port.

98
00:21:30,672 --> 00:21:44,952
PR twenty seven specifies a package-based measurement with an added
two centimetres and a clear depth mark. The catheter must not advance
past that mark. Learn the exact equipment and measurement on the
supervised trainer.

99
00:21:44,952 --> 00:22:01,176
The procedure stops suction when fluid clears or none appears after
fifteen to twenty seconds. It specifies eighty millimetres of mercury,
not generally above one hundred sixty; those are this procedure's
limits, not universal suction settings.

100
00:22:01,176 --> 00:22:14,520
Active vomiting with the i-gel in place requires removal and oral
clearance rather than leaving it in place and continuously suctioning.
Do not reinsert a displaced device while a catheter projects beyond
its tip.

101
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:26,592
A patient who begins to wake may no longer tolerate a supraglottic
airway. Reassess breathing and airway protection rather than waiting
for forceful biting, retching or self-removal.

102
00:22:26,592 --> 00:22:38,736
Prepare suction, a functioning bag and mask, oxygen and assistance
before a planned removal. Use a position and removal method
appropriate to the patient and current procedure.

103
00:22:38,736 --> 00:22:50,496
Communicate with a cooperative patient and remove as actually trained;
the i-gel has no cuff to deflate. Clear secretions and support
breathing immediately if needed.

104
00:22:50,496 --> 00:23:01,032
Continue monitoring after removal. Improved alertness after reversal
medication may be temporary, and the need for airway support can recur
before handover.

105
00:23:01,032 --> 00:23:16,608
An adult has a definite pulse, four shallow breaths per minute and a
wet gurgle. Oxygen raises the displayed saturation to ninety-seven
percent, but the first bagged breath produces almost no chest rise and
an audible leak.

106
00:23:16,608 --> 00:23:31,104
The correct interpretation is continued ventilatory failure, not a
reassuring oxygen number. Clear obstructing fluid, reopen the airway
and establish a two-hand seal with a second provider giving a
controlled breath.

107
00:23:31,104 --> 00:23:46,200
Now compare before and after: is there definite rise, improved air
entry and useful exhaled carbon dioxide? If not, announce persistent
failure, check the next link and request help without pausing all
support.

108
00:23:46,200 --> 00:24:00,216
An SGA becomes a reasoned option only after indication, exclusions,
actual authority and the prior-ventilation requirement are addressed.
Giving an antidote does not replace these airway actions.

109
00:24:00,216 --> 00:24:11,520
Start the station by naming the immediate patient problem, checking
equipment and assigning roles. The observer should be able to hear
which findings justify the selected intervention.

110
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:23,592
Demonstrate opening and clearing the airway, selecting an appropriate
adjunct and producing controlled chest rise with two-provider BVM.
Explain what would make you stop or change the method.

111
00:24:23,592 --> 00:24:39,576
If the instructor provides an indicated SGA scenario, complete
screening, preparation, insertion, confirmation and securement, then
recheck after a simulated move. Do not announce success merely because
insertion is finished.

112
00:24:39,576 --> 00:24:54,768
The instructor then introduces one failure. Identify the changed
finding, correct the relevant link and demonstrate recovery of
effective ventilation. Rehearse until the action and explanation both
meet the actual school rubric.

113
00:24:54,768 --> 00:25:09,192
Report what the patient was doing before intervention, including
pulse, respiratory effort, consciousness and airway contamination.
Without a baseline, the receiver cannot judge the significance of the
current numbers.

114
00:25:09,192 --> 00:25:22,008
State the actual positioning, suction, adjunct and airway device used,
with relevant size, times and attempts. Document a failed attempt as
failed, including the recovery method.

115
00:25:22,008 --> 00:25:35,328
Describe the response with observed chest movement, air entry,
oxygenation and capnography trends. Distinguish an absent waveform
from a low value and report any equipment limitation.

116
00:25:35,328 --> 00:25:50,544
Finish with unresolved risks such as vomiting, recurrent sedation,
difficult seal or displacement during movement, and tell the receiver
which backup is ready. This closes the same assessment-support-
reassessment loop with which the lesson began.
